+# Local voice input
+
+Jarvis voice input uses the Windows offline speech-recognition engine through `System.Speech`. Recorded microphone audio is processed locally and is not sent to a cloud transcription service.
+
+## Controls
+
+- Select **MIC** or press **Ctrl+Space** to begin one voice command.
+- Select **STOP** or press **Ctrl+Space** again to cancel.
+- The cyan meter beneath the composer shows live microphone input.
+- With automatic submission enabled, recognized text is sent immediately. Otherwise it remains in the command box for review.
+- The optional wake phrase listens only while Jarvis is open, idle, and not speaking. Say the wake phrase, wait for the listening indication, and then speak the command.
+
+Voice input and the wake phrase can be configured under **Configure → Voice Input**. Wake-phrase listening is disabled by default.
+
+## Windows prerequisites
+
+1. Allow desktop applications to access the microphone under **Settings → Privacy & security → Microphone**.
+2. Install an English speech language under **Settings → Time & language → Language & region → Language options** if Jarvis reports that no recognizer is installed.
+3. Select the intended default recording device in Windows sound settings.
+
+The first build after applying this change restores the free `System.Speech` NuGet package. Once restored, speech recognition itself works offline.
+
+## Limitations
+
+Windows dictation is suitable for the proof of concept but may be less accurate for technical names than a local Whisper model. The recognition service is isolated behind one class so a Whisper.cpp or faster-whisper provider can replace it later without changing command handling or the avatar state flow.

